%I A397134 #15 Jul 25 2026 18:02:37
%S A397134 37,325669433
%N A397134 Primes p such that p divides the numerator of the Bernoulli number B_{p-5}.
%C A397134 Equivalently, primes p such that (p, p-5) is an irregular pair.
%C A397134 Equivalently, primes p >= 7 such that Sum_{k=1..p-1} 1/k^4 == 0 (mod p^2). By Glaisher's congruence this sum == (4/5)*p*B_{p-5} (mod p^2), so it is always divisible by p, and divisible by p^2 exactly for the terms of this sequence. This is the direct analog of the Wolstenholme primes A088164, characterized by Sum_{k=1..p-1} 1/k^2 == (2/3)*p*B_{p-3} (mod p^2), which is 0 mod p^2 iff p | B_{p-3}. See Meštrović 2011 (in Links), congruence (22).
%C A397134 In 1857 Kummer proved that p | numerator(B_{p-5}) is a necessary condition for the failure of the first case of Fermat's Last Theorem for the exponent p (see Ribenboim, 13 Lectures, p. 29).
%C A397134 a(2) = 325669433 was extracted from the tables of irregular pairs for p < 2^31 of Hart, Harvey, and Ong, then confirmed independently of those tables by verifying Sum_{k=1..p-1} 1/k^4 == 0 (mod p^2) directly. There are no other terms below 2^31 = 2147483648.
%D A397134 J. W. L. Glaisher, On the residues of the sums of products of the first p-1 numbers, and their powers, to modulus p^2 or p^3, Quart. J. Math. 31 (1900), 321-353.
%D A397134 J. W. L. Glaisher, On the residues of the sums of the inverse powers of numbers in arithmetical progression, Quart. J. Math. 32 (1901), 271-288.
%D A397134 Paulo Ribenboim, 13 Lectures on Fermat's Last Theorem, Springer-Verlag, New York, 1979, p. 29.
%D A397134 Zhi-Hong Sun, Congruences concerning Bernoulli numbers and Bernoulli polynomials, Discrete Appl. Math. 105 (2000), 193-223.
%H A397134 William Hart, David Harvey, and Wilson Ong, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/mcom/3211">Irregular primes to two billion</a>, Math. Comp. 86 (2017), 3031-3049; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.02398">arXiv version</a>, arXiv:1605.02398 [math.NT], 2016.
%H A397134 David Harvey, <a href="https://web.maths.unsw.edu.au/~davidharvey/research/twobillion.tar.bz2">List of irregular pairs</a>, 2017. Download size 557 MB.
%H A397134 Romeo Meštrović, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.3057">Wolstenholme's theorem: Its Generalizations and Extensions in the last hundred and fifty years (1862-2012)</a>, arXiv:1111.3057 [math.NT], 2011. See congruence (22).
%e A397134 37 is a term since 37 divides the numerator of B_32 = -7709321041217/510 = -(37*683*305065927)/510, and 32 = 37 - 5.
%o A397134 (PARI) is(p) = isprime(p) && p>=7 && numerator(bernfrac(p-5))%p==0 \\ direct; feasible only for small p
%o A397134 (PARI) is(p) = isprime(p) && p>=7 && Mod(sum(k=1, p-1, Mod(k, p^2)^-4), p^2)==0 \\ O(p) time, O(1) space, via Glaisher's congruence; reaches a(2) in ~1 min
%Y A397134 Cf. A000928 (irregular primes), A027641 (numerators of Bernoulli numbers), A088164 (Wolstenholme primes, the (p, p-3) analog), A212557 (the (p, p-9) analog), A091887 (irregularity index).
%K A397134 nonn,bref,hard,more,new
%O A397134 1,1
